𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟴/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴, 𝗯𝘂𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁𝘀 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗜𝗻𝗰𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes pod is Running, but the restart count keeps increasing. How will you troubleshoot it?”

𝗜 𝘄𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱𝗻’𝘁 𝗷𝘂𝘀𝘁 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗽𝗼𝗱. I’d first find why the container is restarting.

🔍 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Check READY, STATUS and RESTARTS.

2️⃣ Inspect pod events

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 OOMKilled
🔹 Liveness probe failures
🔹 Failed mounts
🔹 Container errors
🔹 Scheduling/events

3️⃣ Check current & previous logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

--previous is especially useful because the previous container instance may have crashed before you could inspect it.

4️⃣ Check resource usage

kubectl top pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Compare actual usage with CPU/memory limits.

5️⃣ Check health probes

Review:

🔹 Liveness probe
🔹 Readiness probe
🔹 Startup probe
🔹 Initial delay
🔹 Timeout
🔹 Failure threshold

A badly configured liveness probe can cause Kubernetes to repeatedly restart an otherwise working application.

6️⃣ Check recent changes

Investigate recent:

🔹 Deployments
🔹 ConfigMap/Secret changes
🔹 Application changes
🔹 Resource-limit changes
🔹 Dependency failures

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

Restart Count → Events → Previous Logs → Resources → Probes → Configuration → Recent Changes → Root Cause

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Running ≠ Healthy.

A pod can show Running while its container is repeatedly crashing and restarting.
